After playing the game for so long, I finally chose rogues to be the class
that I wanted to ascend first. From that point it took about 3 years to do
it. 

My best advice to you is to get your dagger proficiency up as fast as you 
can and get as many stackable sets of daggers as possible until you get a
good, large working stack (your largest unidentified stack will most likely
be uncursed +0). Don't even bother with dropping them on altars until you
have about 30-40 +0 daggers (including your originals) and are ready to 
begin enchanting them. Identifying their B/U/C status too early can break 
up a possibly larger stack, which may cost you and extra scroll or two for
identifying/enchanting them later. I'll typically rustproof first, then add
on enchantments later (corrosion or rust can reduce a stack). Getting even
a small bit of an enchantment on a stack typically commits me to them and
ends the "collection" phase.

In reality, you probably won't be able to carry more than 20-25 of those
daggers in your inventory, but having the extras can save you in a pinch
if they happen to get gobbled by a metallivore or otherwise lost in lava
or water.

For melee, I think Stormbringer proves to be the best item to go with,
especially in combination with the Master Key, although my first near-
ascension rogue was dual-weaponing with Grimtooth and a silver dagger (a
rare find). Adding a second broadsword (elven. preferably) will make for
some tankier up-front damage. Some people swear by silver weapons in the
endgame, but I really didn't have much problem using Stormbringer alone
in this one. Its really a matter of preference, and I like not having to
spend time on unncessary weapon proficiencies (i.e. saber). Just remember
that you can extend the range of your attacks with your daggers and
soften up anything big coming at you.

4 +7 daggers at expert level.

+    2 damage bonus at expert level
+    7 damage enchantment per dagger
+ 2.25 damage average per dagger(small + large / 2)
x    4 daggers per round
------
    45 points of damage per round of combat

Simply put, use your daggers!


Other than that, standard ascension kit rules apply (MR, reflect, speed,
and so on). Most importantly... stay unburdened! Play around with your
stack size if you are having problems with this and keep your inventory
free of cruft. Things that are useful to have, but you never use are in
fact useless.

Anywho, good luck with your bids for ascension and I hope I'll read up
on your rogue soon.


